No. I did not say that, and "Davies was there" does not change the point.

**What I said.** The plaque is not itself a firsthand account; it is a later commemorative text that may quote one. Corroborating records (arrest logs, payroll, opposing press) belong in the historical assessment of what Davies said — not on the plaque. A plaque is not an argument; it is a citation.

**What "Davies was there" establishes.** That Davies could give firsthand testimony about what he personally saw. It does not establish that:
- the words on the plaque are Davies's words as originally recorded, rather than a later paraphrase or memory of them;
- every statement attributed to him describes something he observed rather than something he was told or inferred;
- the sponsor reproduced the statement whole rather than selectively.

Presence is a precondition for firsthand testimony, not proof that a given text is that testimony.

**What the plaque actually needs.** Not police logs — attribution. The minimum that makes a quoted account usable as evidence rather than as slogan:
- Davies's full name and role (e.g., "striker, Local __");
- the source and date of the quotation (e.g., "from a 1938 affidavit" or "as recorded in a 1979 oral-history interview, [archive]");
- the installing body and year.

Three lines. That is the "asterisk." It costs nothing to the commemorative purpose and is what lets a reader distinguish a documented participant statement from an undocumented one.

**Bottom line.** A plaque quoting Davies can stand as a public record of his account if it says whose words, from where, and when. Without that, it records what the sponsor wanted remembered, and Davies's presence at the event does not convert it into his testimony. If you send the plaque text and sponsor, I will say specifically which of the three attribution elements it has and which it lacks.
